# Lesson 6: the trump lead from the lesson's example auction, turned so the student (South) is on lead.
title: Take away dummy's ruffs
id: L06-08
type: exercise
lesson: 6
concepts: 6.9, 6.1
student: S
board: 8

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  A4     QT53   7542    T83
  E  K962   J8     AJ83    A97
  S  873    A64    KQT9    J52
  W  QJT5   K972   6       KQ64

auction:
  W Pass   = Not enough to open
  N Pass   = Not enough to open
  E 1D     = 13+ points, 4+ diamonds
  S Pass   = Not enough to bid
  W 1H     = 6+ points, 4+ hearts
  N Pass   = Nothing to say
  E 1S     = Four spades too
  S Pass   = Nothing to say
  W 3S     = Four spades, about 10-12 points: inviting game
  N Pass   = Nothing to say
  E 4S     = Accepting the invitation
  S Pass   = Nothing to add
  W Pass   = Done
  N Pass   = Nothing to add

auction recap:
  East opened 1♦ and showed four spades too. West, a passed hand, jumped to
  3♠ to invite game, and East accepted. East is declarer, because East bid
  spades first, and so you, South, will lead to the first trick.

lead planning:
  declarer: 13-18 = East opened 1♦ and rebid 1♠ without jumping: about 13 to 18.
  dummy: 10-12 = West passed at first, then invited game: 10 to 12.
  partner: 0-7 = 40, minus your 10, East's 13 or more and West's 10 or more, leaves at most 7.
  remind: Declarer bid diamonds, and your diamonds are strong. Who will win the diamond tricks?
  remind: Dummy has four trumps. What might declarer do with them?

play:
  1   S S3*  W S5  N SA  E S2
      ok: S
      S3 key: A trump lead. Declarer bid diamonds first, and you hold the diamond honors, so declarer has diamond losers that dummy's trumps could ruff. Every trump you lead takes away one ruff.
      S3 not D: The ♦K is a safe card, but it doesn't spoil declarer's plan: ruffing diamonds in dummy. Which lead takes away dummy's trumps?
      S3 not H: West bid hearts, and leading away from your ♥A against a suit contract can hand declarer a trick.
      S3 not C: Clubs are unbid and safe enough, but they don't stop declarer's plan. What will declarer want to do with dummy's trumps?
      note: Partner wins the ♠A, and sees what you're up to.
  2   N S4  E S9  S S7  W ST
      note: Partner leads a second trump. Dummy started with four; now it has two.
  3   W D6  N D2  E DA  S D9
      note: Declarer starts on the plan: cash the ♦A, then ruff diamonds in dummy.
  4   E D3  S DT  W SJ  N D4
      note: The first ruff.
  5   W C4  N C3  E CA  S C2
      note: Back to declarer's hand with the ♣A.
  6   E D8  S DQ  W SQ  N D5
      note: The second ruff uses dummy's last trump. Declarer still has the ♦J to lose.
  7   W H2  N H3  E HJ  S HA
      note: Declarer leads a heart, and you win the ace.
  8   S DK  W H7  N D7  E DJ
      note: Cash your ♦K: dummy has no trumps left to ruff it.
  9   S S8  W H9  N H5  E SK
      note: Nothing else to cash. A trump is safe.
  10  E C7  S C5  W CK  N C8
      note: The rest are declarer's.
  11  W CQ  N CT  E C9  S CJ
  12  W C6  N HT  E H8  S H4
  13  W HK  N HQ  E S6  S H6

play recap:
  Declarer's plan was to ruff diamonds in dummy. Your trump lead, and
  partner's trump back, left dummy only two trumps, so one diamond loser
  stayed behind for your ♦K. Declarer made exactly ten tricks. With any other
  lead, declarer has time to ruff all three diamond losers and make eleven.
  When the auction tells you dummy's trumps will be used for ruffing, a
  trump lead is often the best start.
